// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/style_modulated_truncation_noise.png)
// result_end
\caption(Modulated: Eight rows, columns psi1/.5/0 with shared first-row noise, ordinary with original per-row noise, and changed noise. Equal psi0 images require both common W and common noise.)
\alttext(An8-by5 grid compares three truncation levels with shared noise and two generation controls with per-row noise.)

Truncation uses a fresh2048-row prior bank(zseed6733) mapped through the final
G. Its center is computed as Double mean(W) then cast back to Float32; there is
no moving average. W_psi=center+psi*(W-center). The shared-noise truncation
columns repeat the first row's noise maps at every row; ordinary/changed-noise
columns retain their original per-row banks. A collapse at psi0 here describes
a controlled input condition, not a fitted model collapse.

> Vary noise after fixing every style to the same center

The next additional control keeps both styles at the estimated center while
retaining independent per-row test noise. After strengths learn, those outputs
need not coincide. It contrasts with the original psi0 column, whose noise is
shared too. Each pair below shows shared-noise psi0 on the left and independent
noise psi0 on the right; eight AdaIN rows precede eight modulated rows.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/style_zero_style_noise.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
namespace n = dm::nn;
namespace m = n::models;
using A     = std::map<std::string, n::Tensor>;
A Read(const std::string& path) {
  A a;
  for (const auto& [key, value] : n::load_npz(path))
    a.emplace(key, value);
  return a;
}
int main() {
  std::vector<n::Tensor> panels;
  for (const std::string v : {"adain", "modulated"}) {
    auto control = Read("build/style_gan/" + v + "/style_controls.npz"),
         extra   = Read("build/style_gan/" + v + "/extra-controls.npz");
    auto shared = control.at("truncated_fake2"), varied = extra.at("zero_style_varying_noise");
    if (n::max(n::abs(varied - n::tile(n::take_rows(varied, {0}), {200, 1, 1, 1}))).item() <= 1e-7)
      throw std::runtime_error("learned noise remains active at psi0");
    for (int row = 0; row < 8; ++row) {
      panels.push_back(n::take_rows(shared, {row}));
      panels.push_back(n::take_rows(varied, {row}));
    }
  }
  n::save_image_grid(
      n::concat(panels, 0), "build/style_zero_style_noise.png", 2, 0, 1, 4, 220 / 255., 8);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/style_zero_style_noise.png)
// result_end
\caption(Both columns fix W to the same variant-specific center. Only the right column has independent per-row noise; source learned strengths remain unchanged. The low-resolution images stay weak.)
\alttext(Sixteen two-panel rows compare repeated-style synthesis under common and independent spatial noise, with AdaIN first and modulated second.)

> Quantify source controls and preserve the limits of the result

Final test D/G are.3452040553/.5124273896 for AdaIN and
.3427920640/.5206323266 for the modulated variant. Maximum style-mixing effects
are.0973485708/.0484675169, and changed-noise effects.0158234239/.0060599446.
Those are pixel changes, not perceptual scores. The modulated doubled-first-gain
control changes the output by at most3e-6 while roughly halving its demodulator;
the guard prevents exact scale invariance. The plot below retains every test
row's maximum pixel change for each of the two controls, sorted independently.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/style_effects.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
namespace n = dm::nn;
namespace m = n::models;
using A     = std::map<std::string, n::Tensor>;
A Read(const std::string& path) {
  A a;
  for (const auto& [key, value] : n::load_npz(path))
    a.emplace(key, value);
  return a;
}
int main() {
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.y_limits(-.005, .18);
  for (int k = 0; k < 2; ++k) {
    std::string v   = k ? "modulated" : "adain";
    auto        out = Read("build/style_gan/" + v + "/outputs.npz"),
         c          = Read("build/style_gan/" + v + "/style_controls.npz");
    auto style      = n::maximum(
        n::abs(out.at("fake") - c.at("mixed_first")),
        n::abs(out.at("fake") - c.at("mixed_second")));
    auto noise = n::abs(out.at("fake") - c.at("changed_noise_fake"));
    auto a     = n::max(n::reshape(style, {200, 64}), {1}).to_vector<double>(),
         b     = n::max(n::reshape(noise, {200, 64}), {1}).to_vector<double>();
    std::sort(a.begin(), a.end());
    std::sort(b.begin(), b.end());
    std::vector<double> row(200);
    std::iota(row.begin(), row.end(), 1.);
    p.lines(
         row,
         a,
         v + " style",
         k ? dm::graphics::RGB{22, 163, 74} : dm::graphics::RGB{37, 99, 235},
         2)
        .lines(
            row,
            b,
            v + " noise",
            k ? dm::graphics::RGB{147, 51, 234} : dm::graphics::RGB{220, 38, 38},
            2);
  }
  p.x_label("Sorted held-out row index; independent sorting per curve")
      .y_label("Maximum absolute pixel change within each8x8 row")
      .title("All200 test rows retain measurable style and learned-noise effects")
      .save_svg("build/style_effects.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/style_effects.svg)
// result_end
\caption(All800 source-derived row effects remain in the four curves. Curves do not pair independently sorted rows and provide no real-image quality metric.)
\alttext(Four200-value sorted curves compare style mixing and changed spatial noise for both variants.)

The independent NumPy oracle checks220384 native parameter partials,
576982 forward values,136 scalar finite differences, all384 Adam transitions
bit exactly and every phase's unselected owners. All89600 resized pixels and
6000 fitting row visits match. Original19 archives contain1743020 exact values;
all792 phase/probe archives add928688 exact values. Six original PNGs, four TSVs
and non-timing metric records match. All latent/noise banks, initial zero-noise
controls, per-layer mixing, common-noise truncation, double-gain control and
validation/final test losses are checked separately.

The weak fixed-final results remain visible. Neither latent controls nor a
small adversarial loss establishes digit fidelity, diversity or coverage.
Canonical models and reusable normalization/modulation live in the C++ core;
Python/R expose those same operations and owning fields. Full independent
language experiment loops remain a separate migration task. Optional development
oracle: scripts/check_style_gan.py; preparation and report execution require
only the native datamunge API and normal C++ report toolchain.
